// expect: 57
// W16 GATE — destructuring parameter = param-as-pattern.
//
// A destructure param (`((a, b)) => …` / `({x, y}) => …` / `([h, ...t]) => …`)
// desugars at fn ENTRY to a fresh-named param plus a `let <pat> = <fresh>`
// prepended to the body (parser.mn pat_param_and_let + bind_param_destructures)
// — byte-identical to a destructuring `let`, the SAME match projection
// desugar_block gives. ONE pattern machinery at let-bind AND param-bind. The
// direct `((a, b)) => …` form is .mn-LEADS: the disposable seed's parser lags
// the param desugar (TESTED: the seed traps on a direct destructure param), so
// the direct value gate lands when the seed catches up (peer
// Hβ.seed.param-as-pattern).
//
// This gate proves the desugar's CONTROL SHAPE self-containedly through the
// seed: bind2's body IS exactly the prepend-let the param desugar emits for a
// param `((a, b))` — `let (a, b) = fresh` — so param-bind == let-bind. A tuple
// (7, 5) binds positionally (a=7 -> *1, b=5 -> *10) => 7 + 50 = 57.

fn bind2(fresh) = {
  // The destructure-let the param desugar prepends for `((a, b))`:
  let (a, b) = fresh
  a * 1 + b * 10
}

fn main(q) = bind2((7, 5))
